course: dictionary
lesson: 14897
title: Formal words: recourse
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Recourse is a formal word, so you will meet it in exams, letters and rules, not in a chatty text message. It means a way of getting help or a solution when you are in trouble. «Without recourse to a calculator, she worked out the sum in her head» tells you she did not use a calculator.
explain: Look for the phrase around the word. «To have recourse to» something means to use it because you need to, often because other ways have failed. «When the rain flooded the road, the villagers had recourse to a boat» means they used a boat because they had to get across.
explain: Adults also use recourse when they talk about money and rights. «If the shop sells you a broken toy, your recourse is to ask for your money back» means asking for your money back is the way to put the wrong right. Check the clues: is someone in trouble, and is there a way out? If yes, recourse is probably the word you need.
fact: Recourse means a way of getting help or a solution when you are in trouble.
fact: «To have recourse to» something means to use it because you need to.
fact: «Without recourse to» something means without using it.
fact: «Legal recourse» means using the law to put a wrong right.
fact: Recourse is a formal word, so it is most common in exams, letters and rules.
exercise: «Without recourse to a calculator, she worked out the sum in her head.» What does this mean? | The calculator was broken | She forgot the answer | She was allowed to use a calculator | *She did not use a calculator
exercise: «When the rain flooded the road, the villagers had recourse to a boat.» What does had recourse to mean? | They forgot about the boat | They painted the road | *They used a boat because they needed one | They sold the boat
exercise: Which word means a way of getting help when you are in trouble? | Picnic | *Recourse | Slipper | Whisper
test: «The shop would not give me a refund, so my only recourse was to write a letter.» What is recourse in this sentence? | A long queue at the shop | A holiday | *A way of getting help to put a wrong right | A kind of paper
test: Mum said, «We have no recourse but to walk home.» What does she mean? | We will fly home | *Walking is the only way left, because nothing else will work | We are walking for fun | We lost our way
exam: «The builder broke Sam's fence, and Sam asked him to pay, but he refused.» Sam's legal recourse was to ask a judge to decide. What does legal recourse mean? | Building a new fence | Eating lunch in a courtroom | *Using the law to put a wrong right | Asking a friend to help
